        <div class="cell large-10 medium-10 small-12">
            <h2>Why choose UT Health San Antonio for total joint care?</h2>
            <p>We are one of the nation’s top academic <a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/orthopaedics">orthopaedic programs</a> and one of the largest orthopaedics groups in South Texas. Adults and children from around the world come to us for total joint care.</p>
            <h3>Full spectrum of joint replacement procedures</h3>
            <p>We offer all types of joint replacement surgery (called arthroplasty) to ease pain and help you stay active and independent. We commonly perform replacements for all joints, including:</p>
            <div class="indent-list">
                <ul>
                    <li>Ankles</li>
                    <li>Elbows</li>
                    <li>Hips</li>
                    <li>Knees</li>
                    <li>Shoulders</li>
                    <li>Thumbs</li>
                    <li>Wrists</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <h3>Orthopaedic surgery experts</h3>
            <p>Our team trains other orthopaedic surgeons from all over the globe on innovative and minimally invasive techniques for joint replacements. As an academic health center, we also help train the next generation of orthopaedic surgeons. </p>
            <p>Our surgical specialties include:</p>
             <div class="indent-list">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Minimally invasive surgeries</strong>: When possible, our surgeons use minimally invasive and <a>robotic surgical techniques</a> to replace joints. These surgeries take place through smaller incisions. That leads to less muscle and tissue damage, which makes for an easier, less painful recovery.</li>
                    <li><strong>Revision surgeries</strong>: We’re a leading referral center for patients who need repeat joint replacements. You may need revision surgery if you have complications or poor results from a procedure performed at a different center, or when an implant wears out due to age and use.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Innovative surgical technology</h3>
            <p>We use the latest technological advancements to tailor surgery for your unique anatomy. Depending on your treatment plan, you may benefit from:</p>
            <div class="indent-list">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Advanced computer technology</strong>: When appropriate, our surgical teams use computer-assisted navigation systems and artificial intelligence (AI)-enhanced technology to plan and perform complex joint replacements and revision surgeries.</li>
                    <li><strong>Robot-assisted procedures</strong>: Our orthopaedic surgeons use robotic technology to perform some minimally invasive joint procedures, such as knee replacements. This technology helps your surgeon better position the implant so it feels more natural.</li>
                    <li><strong>3D-printed customized implants</strong>: To ensure your new joint lasts for a long time, we use implants made from the most durable materials available. In some situations, we may use 3D printing technology to customize an implant for your specific body and needs.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Presurgical care </h3>
            <p>Our presurgical care helps optimize your physical health before surgery, which can make recovery easier. These services can lower your risk of complications and include:</p>
            <div class="indent-list">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Perioperative optimization clinic</strong>: Our perioperative team schedules <a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/85,P00208">heart tests</a> and other diagnostics to ensure it’s safe for you to get surgery. When needed, our team gets you in to see specialists who can manage <a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/85,P00224">high blood pressure</a>, <a href="https://lsom.uthscsa.edu/center-excellence-diabetes/">diabetes</a> and other conditions that can affect surgery and recovery. </li>
                    <li><strong>Prehabilitation services</strong>: You may participate in prehabilitation (prehab) physical therapy before surgery to help strengthen the muscles that will support your new joint. The exercises you learn are also helpful during recovery.</li>
                    <li><strong>Services to quit smoking</strong>: Nicotine and tobacco products affect blood flow, which can slow the healing process. Experts in our <a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/services/tobacco-cessation-program">Tobacco Cessation Program</a> provide tools and support to help you quit.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <h3>Postsurgical support</h3>
            <p>We offer dedicated postsurgical services to help you get the most benefit from your new joint. These services include:</p>
            <h2>Do you need joint replacement?</h2>
            <p>Your healthcare provider may refer you to an orthopaedic surgeon if nonsurgical treatments don’t ease joint pain, stiffness and mobility issues. To determine whether joint replacement is right for you, our orthopaedic surgeon will perform a physical exam to assess your joint symptoms and range of motion. </p>
            <p>They may also order one or more tests, such as:</p>
            <div class="indent-list">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/92,P07646">X-rays</a></strong> to look for joint damage</li>
                    <li><strong><a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/92,P07652">MRI</a></strong> or <strong><a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/92,P07649">CT scan</a></strong> to view the joint and connective tissues, such as tendons and ligaments</li>
                    <li><strong>Ultrasound</strong> to look for connective tissue damage</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <h2>Treatments we offer</h2>
            <p>Our orthopaedic surgeons are experts at minimally invasive, partial and total joint replacements, including the following.</p>
            <h3>Hip and knee replacements</h3>
            <p>Our <a>hip and knee replacement</a> surgeons are known for their expertise in performing minimally invasive procedures, often using robot-assisted technology. They replace hundreds of hip and knee joints every year with outstanding results.</p>
            <h3>Shoulder and elbow replacements</h3>
            <p>Our world-renowned <a>shoulder and elbow replacement program</a> reflects the legacy of surgical pioneer <a href="https://news.uthscsa.edu/in-memoriam-charles-a-rockwood-jr-md-legendary-orthopaedic-educator-and-ems-founder-2/">Charlie Rockwood, M.D.</a> During his tenure at UT Health San Antonio, Dr. Rockwood developed novel prosthetic shoulder implants and techniques still in use today. We offer all types of procedures, including linked and unlinked total elbow replacement, reverse total shoulder replacements and shoulder resurfacing.</p>
            <h3>Thumb replacements</h3>
            <p>Our <a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/services/hand-microsurgery">orthopaedic hand surgeons</a> were among the first in the country to offer <a>thumb replacement surgery</a>. This innovative treatment for severe thumb arthritis helps preserve mobility and grip strength. Compared to traditional surgery for thumb arthritis, thumb joint replacement allows you to resume activities faster — typically within six weeks.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
